AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) and Digital Twin (DT) technologies are transforming healthcare delivery and management that enable real-time monitoring and predictive diagnosis, as well as personalized treatment. However, the sudden proliferation of AI-driven systems in hospitals raises important ethical challenges involving issues of data privacy, fairness, explainability, and accountability. Many of the existing AI solutions work as non-transparent black-box models and hinder clinician trust in the applicability in sensitive medical contexts. To overcome these challenges, this study introduces a Federated and Explainable Ethical Digital Twin Framework for smart hospitals. The framework combines IoT-enabled data acquisition, Federated Learning (FL) to enable decentralized and privacy-preserving model training, Explainable AI (XAI) to support the interpretability of diagnostic outputs, and blockchain-based audit trails to provide transparency, security, and accountability. The proposed layered architecture is based on implementation of ethical principles of governance - in accordance with standards of International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC) P7000, as well as in accordance with standards of World Health Organization (WHO) - at all stages of data processing and decision making. A case study into the monitoring of ICU patients demonstrates the potential of the system to produce interpretable alerts on the monitoring of patient vitals and important events such as cardiac arrest, with superior predictive accuracy (92%), high clinician interpretability (4.7/5) and conformance to privacy standards that traditional AI models do not. These results demonstrate that responsible AI that is ethically aligned is compatible with high-performance outcomes in health care environments. The framework helps to build the basis of trustworthy, transparent and sustainable digital twin ecosystems, which are fueling a new paradigm of responsible innovation in predictive and preventive medicine.
