AB  - This study proposes a Reinforcement Learning (RL)-based Personalised Path Planning Algorithm (PPPA). Our approach merges pedagogical constructs with explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) to enhance the interpretability and theoretical alignment of adaptive learning systems. The proposed method addresses the critical challenge of reconciling data-driven optimization with domain-specific educational principles, which often remain opaque in conventional RL approaches. To achieve this, our framework introduces three core advancements. First, a Pedagogical Construct Embedding Module (PCEM) translates learning theories into measurable reward elements. Second, an agent employing Hierarchical Proximal Policy Optimization (HPPO) operates on a dual timescale to harmonize short-term and extended learning objectives. Finally, an XAI-Driven Reward Decomposition Interface (XRDI) delivers live graphical displays that visualize the pedagogical influences on decision-making processes. The PCEM dynamically adjusts action recommendations based on weighted constructs, such as skill progression pacing and error correction sensitivity, while the HPPO agent hierarchically coordinates meta-level goals with granular actions. Furthermore, the XRDI applies SHAP values to decompose rewards into contributions tied to specific constructs, thereby assisting educators in verifying and improving system behavior. Experiments show that the PPPA outperforms baseline methods in both learning efficacy and interpretability, especially in situations demanding adherence to instructional design principles. Ultimately, this work is significant because it concurrently addresses algorithmic efficiency and human-AI cooperation, bridging the gap between RL-driven personalization and pedagogical clarity.  © 2025 IEEE.
