AB  - Artificial intelligence writing tools are revolutionizing EFL grammar instruction, yet their impact diverges at a critical self-efficacy threshold. This study establishes T=3.278 as the pivotal point triggering dual effects: learners above this threshold demonstrate significant grammatical competence growth through strategic metacognitive engagement, effectively harnessing AI as a rule-discovery scaffold. Conversely, learners below this threshold exhibit accelerated grammatical competence erosion, with 60.32% showing dependency-driven regression. This polarized impact necessitates distinct pedagogical responses. Our efficacy-threshold-mediated framework proposes: (1) deliberate metalinguistic practice to amplify high-efficacy learners’ internalization capabilities; (2) progressive tech-decoupling protocols that dynamically adjust AI withdrawal intensity based on real-time diagnostics for low-efficacy cohorts. By transforming static tool evaluation into adaptive scaffolding responsive to learner thresholds, this research provides educators with an evidence-based paradigm to resolve East Asia’s enduring EFL dilemma—balancing technological efficiency against grammatical sustainability. The implications extend to intelligent tutoring systems and learning analytics frameworks seeking to optimize human-AI pedagogical partnerships. © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s)
