AB  - Technology is altering everything in our lives, and right now the school system is going through one of the largest shifts in its history. Artificial intelligence (AI) is transforming how we think about things, particularly in science, technology, engineering, and math (STEM) education, which prizes a thorough understanding of topics, the ability to solve problems, and logical reasoning. Traditional means of teaching don't always work to help each student reach their individual learning objectives, especially in large, diverse courses. In this instance, programs that use AI could be useful. The purpose of this study is to find out how AI-powered intelligent tutoring systems could make high school STEM education better. Adaptive content delivery, real-time feedback, and tailored learning paths are some of the ways these technologies benefit students that even the finest instructors may not always be able to do on their own. Using a comparative research technique, we look at the academic performance, levels of engagement, and progress in each subject of two hundred students in both traditional and AI-enhanced settings. Students that used AI tutors were more interested, involved, and sure in their ability to solve problems. They also scored better on assessments. These findings are very impressive. The results of this research suggest that AI doesn't replace instructors; it helps them perform their jobs better. Because of this, STEM education is better for the future, more successful, and more available to everyone. © 2025 IEEE.
