AB  - Money laundering poses a persistent global threat, undermining both financial stability and the sound operation of economic systems. One of the central obstacles in advancing Anti-Money Laundering (AML) methods is balancing strict data confidentiality with the necessity of analyzing large, interconnected financial transaction networks. This paper introduces the first comparative assessment of centralized Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) and privacypreserving Federated Learning (FL)-based GNNs for AML detection, evaluated on the SAML dataset. Our study contrasts the performance of federated GNNs with centralized counterparts and with traditional baselines, including rulebased models, random forests, and autoencoders. Results indicate that federated GNNs deliver strong detection accuracy while safeguarding sensitive information, though with tradeoffs involving efficiency, scalability, and regulatory alignment. These outcomes provide a foundation for designing future AML frameworks that balance predictive effectiveness with privacy preservation. Additionally, the study shows how collaborative analytics and immutable auditability can coexist with compliance constraints by integrating these architectures into permissioned blockchain environments like Hyperledger Fabric. The integration of AI-driven, privacy-conscious AML solutions into regulated financial ecosystems is made possible by this work, which bridges the gap between automation, interpretability, and practical implementation. © 2025 IEEE.
