AB  - This study analyzes the development of an augmented intelligence framework for emotion detection and proposes an augmented intelligence framework for emotion detection that integrates Explainable AI (XAI) to enhance interpretability for non-expert users. Using the FER-2013 and RAVDESS datasets were classified into one of the 7 basic emotions using four machine learning models: Random Forest, K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), and Support Vector Machine (SVM). Data repositories, including Scopus, Web of Science, and IEEE Xplore, were systematically reviewed to contextualize findings and inform framework design. Model evaluation revealed that Random Forest consistently outperformed other algorithms across both datasets, achieving the highest average F1-scores (0.375 on FER-2013 and 0.585 on RAVDESS), particularly in detecting emotions such as anger and calmness. Results demonstrate a significant contribution from select features in the Random Forest model, especially feature 2230 in FER2013 and feature 0 in RAVDESS, towards accurate emotion classification, with notable accuracy metrics for emotions such as anger and calmness These results informed the design of a human-in-the-loop (HITL) augmented intelligence framework, aimed at improving model adaptability and robustness in uncontrolled environments The establishment of ethical frameworks for data privacy and user consent related to responsible innovation and well-being is emphasized in the policy implications. The study highlights the delicate balance between personalized emotion detection and privacy concerns, emphasizing the importance of robust data governance to maintain user trust. The proposed augmented intelligence framework focuses on transparent and accurate affective computing applications, particularly in healthcare, education, and human-computer interaction domains. ©2025 IEEE.
