AB  - This chapter examines the role of blockchain as a transformative instrument in the digital energy transition, highlighting its capacity to support decentralised trading, automate compliance, and enhance institutional transparency. It analyses how blockchain aligns with smart-governance principles by embedding regulatory logic into digital infrastructures while requiring adaptive legal frameworks to address liability, data protection, and market oversight. Through comparative case studies—including Brooklyn Microgrid, Powerledger, and WePower—the chapter illustrates the interplay between technological innovation, regulatory experimentation, and policy integration. Ethicaldimensions suchas fairness, environmental sustainability, and algorithmic accountability are addressed as essential conditions for legitimate decentralisation. The findings demonstrate that blockchain’s effectiveness depends not on technological capability alone but on the coherence of governance, legal interoperability, and societal inclusion. © 2026, IGI Global Scientific Publishing. Copying or distributing in print or electronic forms without written permission of IGI Global Scientific Publishing is prohibited. Use of this chapter to train generative artificial intelligence (AI) technologies is expressly prohibited.
