AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) has become an argumentative concern in educational contexts. This phenomenon offers personalization, adaptive tutoring, and real-time feedback for learners. However, its widespread use has triggered deep ethical and privacy concerns. This chapter reports these concerns by tracing how AI reshapes pedagogy, governance, and integrity in education. Taking the stance of community-wide ethical frameworks (Holmes etal., 2022)and bibliometric mappings of philosophical and practical principles of Yu & Yu (2023), it situates AI ethics within deontological, utilitarian, and virtue-ethical orientations that emphasize transparency, fairness, accountability, and non-maleficence. Particular attention is given to privacy as a pedagogical and policy issue. The datafication of learning, surveillance-based analytics, and the opacity of commercial EdTech infrastructures challenge both institutional trust and learners’ autonomy (Ryan et al., 2021; Kousa & Niemi, 2023). In response, the chapter advances the notion of privacy literacy, the capacity of teachers and students to understand, question, and ethically manage data practices embedded in AI-mediated learning (Neff et al., 2024; Aljabr & Al-Ahdal, 2024). Parallel to privacy risks, the expansion of generative systems such as ChatGPT has destabilized conventional understandings of authorship and academic honesty. Emerging concepts like AI-giarism expose new grey zones between assistance and misconduct (Cotton et al., 2023; Chan, 2025; Goddiksen et al., 2024). The chapter concludes by proposing an ethically resilient educational model that integrates critical AI literacy (Walter, 2024) and co-responsibility among teachers, students, and systems. Ethical resilience refers to the capacity of educational communities to adapt responsibly to technological change through privacy-by-design architectures, algorithmic explainability, and participatory policy-making. Finally, to safeguard integrity and privacy in AI-mediated education, stakeholders should seek regulatory oversight with the creation of transparent, equitable, and culturally responsive contexts. Thus, humans may not have devolved their positions to algorithms at the ethical core of digital learning. Copyright © 2026, IGI Global Scientific Publishing.
