AB  - This work-in-progress paper presents the design and early development of an AI-driven adaptive learning system for introductory programming education grounded in established pedagogical theories, notably Bloom's taxonomy and Vygotsky's Zone of Proximal Development. The system integrates interpretable learner modeling (BKT, IRT) with data-driven approaches (DKT) and adaptive decision-making, exploring reinforcement learning for task sequencing and scaffolded feedback. A conversational tutoring agent based on fine-tuned open-source LLMs (e.g., Llama, Mistral) provides context-aware guidance while operating within pedagogically constrained prompts aligned to course objectives. The platform captures multi-modal interaction data (code submissions, hint usage, assessments) to estimate concept mastery across cognitive levels and to personalize recommendations in real time. Instructor-facing learning analytics dashboards surface explainable indicators of student progress and misconceptions to support targeted interventions. We outline the system architecture, data collection plan, experimental design for pilot studies (A/B and within-subject comparisons), and evaluation metrics spanning learning gains, model adaptivity, transparency, and user experience. Expected contributions include a theory-aligned adaptive framework for programming education, a functional prototype leveraging open-source components for institutional control and equity, and empirical insights into the effectiveness and explainability of LLM-supported tutoring. Preliminary implementation status and forthcoming pilots will inform iterative, design-based refinements toward scalable, ethical, and inclusive intelligent tutoring in engineering education. © 2026 IEEE.
