AB  - Artificial intelligence in education is now being talked about in the context of personalization, conversational support, automated assessment, and institutional adoption, but the entrepreneurial and cybersecurity aspects of the developments are not fully incorporated. Relying on a hierarchical narrative analysis of thirty-seven sources on the subject that use domains, this chapter combines learning analytics, adaptive gamification, domain-focused knowledge graphs, chatbot mediation, micro-credentialing, and governance constraints to form a single pedagogical structure. successful tutoring of cybersecurity entrepreneurship ought to be artifact-based, contextualized to startups, legally conscious, and analytics-intensive since startups and early-stage teams ought to be trained to prioritize threats, justify controls, communicate residual risk, and productize trust in harsh conditions of uncertainty. As a result, an assessment logic based on rubrics, and an implementation roadmap are elaborated to facilitate human-assisted, AI-enhancedlearningofcyberrisksintechnologystartups. © 2026 by IGI Global Scientific Publishing. All rights reserved. No part of this publication may be reproduced,  stored or distributed in any form or by any means, electronic or mechanical, including photocopying, without written permission  from the publisher. Use of this publication to train generative artificial intelligence (AI) technologies is expressly prohibited.  The publisher reserves all rights to license its use for generative AI training and machine learning model development.
