AB  - Teaching computer science at undergraduate and graduate levels involves working with students ranging from coding novices to experienced programmers, large class sizes, and a shift toward online and hybrid classrooms. These conditions often prevent instructors from providing prompt, personalized feedback without compromising quality. Recent progress in AI-powered educational tools (Holstein & Aleven, 2019; Swamy et al., 2024), including intelligent tutoring systems (ITS), automated assessment, and adaptive dashboards, offers instructors practical support for lesson planning, teaching, and evaluation. This paper examines how Explainable AI (XAI) can be integrated into the teaching processes to reduce workload, enhance professional development, and support reflective practice. The proposed framework also emphasizes the role of collaborative faculty communities and considers broader implications for curriculum design, online pedagogy, and computer-assisted learning in higher education. © ML ICE du Tech 2026.
