AB  - Driven by the dual forces of educational informatization 2.0 and the reform of university English teaching, in order to address the shortcomings of traditional flipped classrooms in personalized teaching, interaction efficiency, and evaluation accuracy, this study focuses on the perspective of AI empowerment, exploring the reconstruction path and practical effectiveness of the flipped classroom teaching model for university English. Based on constructivism and personalized learning theories, this study constructed a closed-loop teaching model of "AI intelligent preview-enhanced interactive classroom-AI personalized consolidation". Through a 16-week quasi-experimental study of two parallel classes (experimental class of 52 students, control class of 50 students) in a certain university, using a combination of quantitative and qualitative methods to test the effectiveness. The results showed that the improvement in English listening, speaking, reading, and writing scores of the experimental class was significantly higher than that of the control class (p < 0.05), with an average increase of 12.3 points in oral language scores; the score of the learning motivation scale in the intrinsic goal orientation dimension (M = 4.21, SD = 0.58) was significantly higher than that of the control class (M = 3.67, SD = 0.62), and the planning and reflective thinking degree of autonomous learning ability improved particularly significantly; the teacher feedback indicated that AI technology could reduce 60% of mechanical work and enhance the targeted nature of teaching. From a computing-Technology perspective, we implement the proposed teaching workflow as an AI-enabled education service pipeline. Specifically, (i) a learning-Analytics data stream collects LMS click logs, quiz records, and classroom interaction traces and normalizes them into session-level feature vectors; (ii) an NLP module (Transformer-based semantic encoder) supports pre-class preview by generating topic summaries, vocabulary scaffolds, and diagnostic quizzes; (iii) an in-class interaction engine provides intent detection for student questions and real-Time retrieval of micro-resources; and (iv) a post-class adaptive consolidation module combines mastery estimation with rule-constrained recommendation to generate individualized practice sets and feedback. These components are deployed as lightweight services (API-based), enabling reproducible integration with common campus LMS platforms and producing machine-logged evidence for later effectiveness analysis. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
