AB  - Modern AI assistants prioritize friction-free retrieval, potentially inducing cognitive offloading and reducing critical engagement with information. Prism is an AI-powered learning companion that treats every user query as an opportunity for structured cognitive engagement. Rather than provide an immediate friction-free response, it follows up with questions to prompt the user to think more deeply about their question. These targeted Socratic follow-up questions create an interactive forum. A companion real-time dashboard - built on a proprietary cognitive framework mapping critical thinking dimensions to Explainable AI (XAI) categories (informed by related work on question-driven reflection in machine-assisted decision-making [1]) - scores each user's reasoning profile across four practitioner archetypes and surfaces nudges toward cognitive moves they have not yet made. This paper contributes (1) a novel interaction design for intentional cognitive speed bumps in professional AI tools, (2) a novel cognitive-XAI mapping framework that inverts the conventional use of explainability categories: rather than making AI reasoning transparent to users, XAI dimensions serve as the scoring substrate for evaluating and developing the user's own reasoning, and (3) early evidence that frictional design is both viable and welcomed in high-stakes knowledge work contexts.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
