AB  - Based on Cognitive Load Theory (CLT) and Contingency Theory, this study constructs a theoretical model examining how Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) application patterns influence learning engagement, with particular emphasis on the moderating effects of metacognitive ability and disciplinary type (science/engineering vs. humanities). Data were collected from 727 undergraduate students in industry-oriented universities (effective response rate: 88.2%), with 52.2% in technical majors, 40.4% in applied engineering, and 7.4% in humanities. Structural Equation Modeling (SEM) and hierarchical regression analysis were employed for hypothesis testing. Key technical findings: (1) Metacognition-guided usage (active inquiry, critical integration) demonstrated significantly higher cognitive engagement than heuristic-dependent usage (3.85 vs. 3.15, t=9.637, p<0.001), validating the cognitive scaffolding empowerment pathway; (2) An inverted U-shaped nonlinear relationship was confirmed between usage frequency and learning engagement (quadratic term β =-0.0072, R2=0.0013, p<0.05), with optimal engagement at 3-4 sessions per week; (3) Metacognitive ability positively moderated pattern effects (high-metacognition group: effect size 0.571 vs. low group: 0.420, ΔR2=0.021, p<0.001); (4) Disciplinary differences were significant: applied engineering (0.582) > technical (0.562) > humanities (0.851, n=31). Technical contributions: This study provides algorithmic governance evidence for GenAI deployment in higher education informatization, proposing a human-AI collaborative learning design framework with practical value for Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) development. This aligns with ITS effectiveness research, which establishes that adaptive systems with mixed-initiative dialogue capabilities yield comparable learning gains to human tutoring when properly calibrated to learner characteristics. Emerging AI education frameworks further corroborate our findings regarding the contingent role of disciplinary contexts in optimizing human-AI collaboration, while educational cobots and smart classroom implementations extend the practical applicability of our proposed usage pattern taxonomy. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
