AB  - Intelligent tutoring systems (ITS) for less-commonly-taught languages such as Spanish face critical challenges of learner data sparsity and cognitive model cold-start, whereas English-based ITS have accumulated large-scale, well-annotated learner behavior datasets over decades of development. To bridge this gap, this paper proposes a Cross-Language Teaching Paradigm Transfer (CLPT) framework. CLPT first distills empirically validated learning behavior features from mature English ITS, including sentence-parsing dwell-time patterns, vocabulary forgetting trajectories, and gamified quiz response characteristics. A domain-adaptive algorithm then aligns these features with Spanish-specific linguistic attributes, covering verb conjugation complexity, grammatical gender agreement, and subjunctive mood discrimination. On this basis, the framework constructs a knowledge tracing model tailored to Spanish learners and realizes cross-lingually calibrated adaptive recommendation of personalized practice resources. The framework is deployed on two widely used educational platforms, Xuexitong and Kahoot, and validated with authentic classroom interaction data from 126 undergraduate Spanish majors over two semesters. Experimental results show that, compared with baseline models built from scratch, CLPT accelerates cognitive model convergence by 45%, achieves a personalized resource recommendation accuracy of 89.2% on the Xuexitong platform, increases student engagement in Kahoot quiz sessions by 37.6%, and improves overall Spanish productive competence by 21.4% in international communication simulation tasks. These findings validate the effectiveness of cross-lingual teaching data transfer and offer a scalable, data-efficient paradigm for constructing ITS for low-resource languages. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
