AB  - The widespread adoption of networked learning systems has enabled large-scale data collection and real-time monitoring of student interactions over connected educational platforms. In such environments, data-driven analytics services play a critical role in supporting adaptive learning and timely pedagogical interventions. This paper investigates the effectiveness of several machine learning algorithms for student performance prediction using data collected from a Digital SAT (DSAT) assessment conducted in a fully digital and networked learning environment. A dataset comprising responses from 273 high school students across grades 10, 11, and 12 is analyzed. Logistic Regression, Random Forest, Support Vector Machine (SVM), and k-Nearest Neighbors (k-NN) are evaluated using a stratified five-fold cross-validation strategy and standard performance metrics, including accuracy, macro-averaged precision, recall, and F1-score. Experimental results demonstrate that non-linear and ensemble-based models outperform linear approaches, with Random Forest achieving the best overall performance. The results highlight the potential of ensemble learning models as data-driven analytics services for networked learning systems, enabling scalable and timely student performance prediction to support intelligent tutoring and adaptive educational services. © 2026 IEEE.
