AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) applications are rapidly reshaping university teaching through adaptive tutoring, generative dialogue, automated feedback, learning analytics, predictive support, and AI-assisted assessment. Yet the educational value of these applications depends not merely on access to technology but on how they are embedded in pedagogy. This study examines the impact of AI applications on university teaching quality and students’ self-directed and self-regulated learning skills. It uses a systematic integrative review and a secondary quantitative synthesis of verified, peer-reviewed evidence. The principal quantitative corpus comprises 85 higher-education studies, 387 dependent effect-size estimates, and 9,564 participants published between 2015 and 2025. Descriptive analyses were reconstructed from publicly available supplementary data, while inferential conclusions were based on published three-level and random-effects meta-analytic estimates; no synthetic data were used. AI feedback had a medium positive average effect on self-regulated learning (Hedges’ g = 0.509), but its 95% prediction interval ranged from −0.661 to 1.678, indicating that effects are highly conditional. Generative-AI interventions also improved academic performance (g = 0.683, 95% CI [0.17, 1.19]), while ChatGPT-supported learning improved behavioral, cognitive, and emotional engagement. However, heterogeneity was substantial, AI feedback did not clearly outperform human feedback, and experimental evidence identified risks of dependence, shallow processing, and “metacognitive laziness.” Teacher support emerged as a decisive pedagogical condition in the academic-performance literature: teacher-supported student–AI interaction produced a large effect (g = 1.426), whereas unsupported use produced a near-zero effect (g = 0.077). The study concludes that AI can improve university teaching quality and learner autonomy when it functions as a scaffold within teacher-designed learning, not as a substitute for disciplinary teaching, human feedback, reflective judgment, or independent performance. © 2026 Slovenian Sociological Association. All rights reserved.
