AB  - Virtual environments for procedural training enable students to practice complex tasks within safe and controlled settings. In this context, anticipating student actions is essential for providing timely and adaptive feedback. This study compares a previously proposed automaton-based model with state-of-the-art supervised machine learning approaches. Several models were evaluated, including Gradient Boosting, Support Vector Machines, Random Forests, Transformer-based neural networks, and a GRU architecture. Given the class imbalance in the dataset, class-weighting techniques and Bayesian optimization were used to enhance the prediction of pedagogically significant errors. Furthermore, a meta-classifier was developed to integrate the outputs of the most promising models. The results indicate that this approach outperforms the automaton in overall accuracy, particularly in the detection of learning-critical errors. The integration of such models into intelligent tutoring systems is discussed, highlighting their potential to enhance educational experiences in virtual environments for procedural training. © 2026 The Author(s). Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license. http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/
