AB  - Practice-oriented learning plays a critical role in labor education and comprehensive practical courses, particularly in cultivating procedural skills, operational norms, and safety awareness. However, teachers often face difficulties in providing continuous and individualized guidance during hands-on activities due to time and attention constraints. Intelligent Tutoring Systems (ITS) have demonstrated effectiveness in personalized learning support and data-driven instructional decision-making, yet their application in practice-intensive learning environments remains limited.This paper proposes an integrated framework that couples ITS with innovative learning spaces to enable step-level sensing, dynamic mastery estimation, and layered interventions while preserving teacher authority. Grounded in a Cognition-Practice-Field theoretical model, the framework integrates multimodal process sensing, step-level knowledge tracing (dual-tier Bayesian+neural model), confidence-weighted multimodal fusion, and a calibrated intervention scoring mechanism. A practical staged deployment strategy and quasi-experimental evaluation plan are provided to validate feasibility and effectiveness. The study contributes theoretical clarity, implementable computational models, and operational guidance for scaling intelligent instructional support in practice-oriented educational settings.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
