AB  - In smart city governance, data-driven social welfare resource allocation systems require subsidized housing programs. Most artificial intelligence systems excel at delivering accurate predictions but lack the fairness, explainability, and accountability necessary for public sector decision-making. This research presents an AI framework that provides explainable results and maintains fairness in allocating housing subsidies to eligible recipients, using American Community Survey socio-economic data. The allocation method employs constrained optimization to solve the distribution problem, combining interpretable vulnerability scoring with established fairness requirements for protected demographic groups, and methods that explain decisions. The system incorporates a human-in-the-loop governance layer for auditing and policy development monitoring. An experimental study tests the proposed framework against rule-based allocation, vulnerability-only ranking, and fairness-agnostic machine learning methods. The proposed method achieves maximum vulnerability coverage (↑↑) while sustaining complete budget compliance (BU = √). The approach achieves low demographic parity difference (DPD) and a disparate impact ratio (DIR) near 1, indicating better demographic balance compared to baseline methods, which maintain high DPD and DIR values below 1. The results demonstrate that welfare allocation systems become more equitable and transparent when decision mechanisms exhibit explainability and fairness constraints. © 2026 IEEE.
