AB  - The paper is a systematic review of artificial intelligence based defense against the last mile aggregation attacks, which use the phishing and cross-site scripting (XSS) techniques in order to evade centralized security controls. The review presents empirical and experimental research based on publicly available phishing and XSS data sets, such as email-based phishing sets, malicious URL sets, collection of JavaScript/XSS payloads and newer data sets based on artificial generalization of phishing content. The approach of systematic comparison is chosen to compare supervised machine learning, deep learning, federated learning, edge AI, and explainable AI (XAI) in terms of detection accuracy, inference latency, and privacy preservation as well as the possibility of operational implementation in resource-constrained endpoints. According to the results of the survey, transformer-based and deep learning models are reliably able to detect complex phishing and XSS patterns with high detection accuracy, but cannot be seen as an adequate choice because of their high computational overheads that do not allow deploying such models in the edge. Conversely, the lightweight and federated learning models have lower latency and better privacy guarantees at the cost of a moderate decrease in detection performance. On the whole, the review reveals the obvious trade-off between accuracy, interpretability and operational efficiency, and the lack of standardized and up-to-date datasets that can simultaneously reflect contemporary phishing and XSS activity. With these results, the article describes the research directions of designing hybrid, privacy-aware, and low-overhead AI designs that would be configured to the last-mile reassembly attacks. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s)
