AB  - With the increasing deployment of machine learning in sensitive domains such as healthcare, ensuring both model utility and privacy has become critical. This work presents a comprehensive experimental framework comparing a Variational Quantum Classifier with classical Logistic Regression and Differentially Private Logistic Regression on the Breast Cancer Wisconsin dataset. This paper generates model explanations using SHapley Additive exPlanations and evaluates privacy leakage using two complementary membership inference attacks: Yeom’s loss-threshold attack and Shokri’s shadow-model attack. The results reveal that Differentially Private Logistic Regression mitigates membership leakage at the cost of slight utility reduction, while Variational Quantum Classifier achieves comparable classification performance with promising privacy performance due to its latent quantum embeddings. Furthermore, SHapley Additive exPlanations analyses demonstrate that both DP and quantum models preserve privacy, highlighting the trade-offs between utility, explainability, and privacy. This study provides a systematic comparison of classical, Differentially Private Logistic Regression, and quantum models under explainability and privacy evaluation, offering insights into designing privacy-preserving, interpretable quantum machine learning systems. © ICST Institute for Computer Sciences, Social Informatics and Telecommunications Engineering 2027.
